    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some trouble with these walnut dovetails i'm cutting for a cabinet set. the pins are splintering a bit at the very tips when i try to pare them down to size. i've checked my chisels and they feel sharp, but i keep getting these tiny little crushed fibers instead of a clean cut. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my grain management or tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The splintering is likely caused by cutting too deep on the first pass with a dull spot on your chisel edge, forcing the tool to crush the tight end grain rather than slice it. Ensure you take shallow maintenance cuts and focus the tip of your wedge chisel so the space wedge and your sawkerf form a single continuous line from the shoulder down to the bottom rag plane. Additionally, check that you are cutting at the exact same angle as your original sawkerf matches the wedge to prevent binding and induce uneven pressure.",
